      "id": "a01",
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "score": 6,
      "estimated_minutes": 10,
      "reason": "The reviewer must separate the claim that Paragraph A’s factor list is exhaustive from the observation that it addresses only the Policy Date. The comparison turns on whether that observation identifies d02’s allocation of initial and later rates between Paragraphs A and B, despite never identifying Paragraph B’s role."
    },
    {
      "id": "a02",
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "score": 4,
      "estimated_minutes": 6,
      "reason": "The reviewer must distinguish the complaint about unspecified weighting and the relationship between expectations and rates from d03’s exhaustive-versus-illustrative dispute over “based on.” A check of the court’s discussion of omitted formulas also helps delimit the description’s possible connection to d06; the remaining defects are readily separated."
    },
    {
      "id": "a03",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "score": 8,
      "estimated_minutes": 18,
      "reason": "The description identifies the relationship between the two factor lists, but explains it through an earlier-list limitation rather than the court’s initial-rate/subsequent-adjustment division. The reviewer must test that asserted limitation and decide whether the shared relational complaint still identifies d07 when it omits the temporal distinction on which the construction turned."
    },
    {
      "id": "a04",
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "score": 5,
      "estimated_minutes": 7,
      "reason": "The reviewer must determine whether the undefined class boundaries and face-amount/age subgroups identify d09’s post-issuance subdivision problem without expressly mentioning timing. That requires distinguishing class formation from d08’s separate question whether differential treatment has an acceptable underwriting basis."
    },
    {
      "id": "a05",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 1,
      "estimated_minutes": 1,
      "reason": "The description expressly targets “recoup prior losses” and the prospective-versus-retrospective repricing distinction. Those words and that problem are plainly separate from both supplied defects concerning unfair discrimination and class boundaries."
    }
  ]
}
